+# Network failure diagnostics and create-acknowledgement record
+
+**10 November 2026. Offline candidate only; no runtime retry authorized.** Base `58980c698bdfcdee5b2eb68d5c8a7577e6758e4d`. The first approved smoke failed at the exact sysfs listing assertion and was cleaned. This amendment adds evidence collection without changing that admission decision.
+
+Actor: local harness operator on the committed socket/engine. Inputs remain the explicit endpoint only. Image, six-file bundle, Node arguments, filesystem grants, mounts, non-root/capability/resource limits, network none and no-DB/no-SDK scope stay fixed. No new package or operational-resource access.
+
+Before the unchanged `['lo']` assertion, only when the listing differs, capture a tagged stderr diagnostic: up to 16 validated entry names; lstat kind; read-only `flags`, `operstate`, `type` under the existing `/sys/class/net` grant; assigned addresses from Node's standard OS API. Read/permission/missing/unsupported data are explicit unavailable values, never a reason to widen grants. The JSON record is bounded to 8 KiB plus its newline terminator. It is evidence only and cannot authorize an interface or represent a route-table measurement. The collector has explicit read-only injected ports with no live defaults for unit tests. No `/proc` reads, child tools or external network requests are introduced by diagnostics.
+
+The ranked unresolved explanations are a non-device sysfs control entry, inactive kernel-created devices, a difference in observation views, or active unexpected devices. File kind, flags/state and addresses can discriminate some cases. Address-only APIs cannot exclude unaddressed devices; routes and namespace provenance remain unmeasured. A reviewer must decide whether these diagnostics are sufficient for a later admission amendment. No blanket name allowlist or guard relaxation is implemented.
+
+Immediately after validating a full create-acknowledged ID, synchronously write the existing created-record shape to stderr before any post-create Docker info/inspect. Simulated fixtures can supply an explicit test reporter only with their callable executor; reporter validation occurs before resource access, and omission is a no-op only in simulated mode. A reporting failure after create is unresolved and prevents start; an unknown/failed create emits no ID record. The launcher removes its later duplicate record. Printed IDs alone remain insufficient cleanup authority.
+
+The Docker failure-message cap increases from 2,000 to 16,384 characters so one bounded diagnostic plus the assertion fits. This does not change Docker commands or access; diagnostic output remains on stderr and is never accepted as successful scenario JSON. Unit tests verify capture bounds, unavailable data, path validation, strict-guard preservation, reporting before post-create checks, reporting failure and no ID record for unknown creates.
+
+Offline tests/replays are authorized development work. Any future real diagnostic-enabled smoke needs a new reviewed commit/hash inventory and explicit scope. On the previously observed listing it is expected to FAIL again after recording diagnostics; that result is not successful admission. Require A–D source/engine/image/baseline/cleanup proof and stop after any post-create failure. No backend, DB, provider, root-adoption, deployment, full C3/M0 or load acceptance is granted.
+
+Primary references: [Linux sysfs network ABI](https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-class-net), [Node 22.23.4 assigned-address API](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.23.4/doc/api/os.md#osnetworkinterfaces), [Node synchronous filesystem writes](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.23.4/doc/api/fs.md#fswritesyncfd-buffer-offset-length-position). They describe APIs, not the unmeasured state of the failed container.
